Sales Forecast
==============

Sales forecasts for salable goods only, excluding services, based on all sales
except cancelled sales, regardless of shipment state. Quantities are converted
to the product's default unit; sale lines with incompatible unit categories are
omitted and reported to the user.

History updates recalculate from the last historical date through today,
including the entire last day, and preserve earlier dates. Select "Recalculate
Entire History" in the update wizard to rebuild all dates, including backdated
changes or cancellations. The first update always builds the entire history.
The wizard also shows the calculation timestamp of the latest stored history,
separately from the last sales date, to identify when it was calculated.

Calculate forecasts from the main calculation screen. Choose the company,
calendar, date range and optional products, then calculate the desired method.
Calculated results are read-only. Week labels use ISO WW/YY. Historical weekly
comparisons use the same ISO week in the previous year, falling back to week 52
when the previous year has no week 53.

Percentages are entered as percentages in the client (20 means 20%). Existing
stored ratios remain unchanged. A product-specific increase takes precedence
over category increases. Contradictory category increases are rejected. Set the
product family root to limit the suggested categories to your family hierarchy.

Existing historical parameters are reused by default. Select "Refresh Forecast
Parameters" to refresh sales history, distributions and calendar event results.
Missing parameters are initialized automatically, and calendar edits invalidate
the affected cached parameters. Product days are excluded from the weekday
distribution only for the products involved. Special-sale exclusions follow
their product or customer scope.

Weekday distributions cover the last twelve calendar months, including today
and excluding the equivalent date a year ago. Customer-category special sales
exclude products actually sold on the event date, including newly sold products.
Special-sale reference weeks use the same ISO-week rule as General forecasts;
incompatible units are omitted and reported in these calculations too.

Key-account orders and customer truck/pallet quantities retain their weekly
total when distributed over holidays; those explicit quantities take precedence
over calendar event forecasts. Orders include every non-cancelled sale in the forecast week,
using the shipping date or, if absent, the sale date. Confirmation does not
remove an order from the forecast. Mixed forecasts without orders use General
parameters, including family increases, and calendar events. New Product uses
only months closed both at calculation time and before the forecast period. A
missing closed month with sales raises an error instead of producing a zero.
New Product offers a Reference Product source on the product's Planning tab.
This source requires a reference product and uses its average closed months
with history and weekday distribution, applying the new product's increase
percentage. The other sources use the product's own history.
Reference products must have compatible
units; quantities are converted to the new product's default unit.

Manual forecasts preserve their weekly quantity when distributing it over
weekdays and holidays. Partial weeks include only the selected days. Daily
rounding is reconciled with the weekly total. A product day requires an explicit
quantity; zero is allowed. Missing packaging conversion factors are never
replaced by a one-to-one conversion: unavailable boxes/pallets remain empty,
and entering boxes or pallets without the required factors raises an error.

"Forecast vs Actual Sales" compares the latest complete forecast for each
product/week with actual sales, by week or for the entire selected period. It
supports products, family categories, forecast method and units/boxes/pallets.
A missing forecast is shown as N/A, also in aggregates containing a missing
week. An unknown packaging conversion is also N/A. Percentage differences are
N/A when actual sales are zero. Subtotals and a grand total are included.
The product's planning tab also selects the latest complete forecast per week;
recalculating one week keeps the latest results for the other weeks visible.

The customer integration initializes the family root from Familia/Família and
builds calendar product days from sales of products in Diadas/Diades. Dates with
manual edits are preserved when regenerating automatic calendar entries.

Planning displays year-to-date growth versus the same dates last year, through
yesterday, and previous-month growth versus the same full month last year, from
company-specific sales history: (latest - previous) / previous. Current partial
periods are excluded. A zero or negative comparison base leaves the trend empty;
a positive base with no subsequent sales gives -100%. These indicators do not
change forecast increase percentages. Refresh sales history to update the data.

The calculation form also displays aggregate annual and monthly growth for the
selected products (all eligible products when empty), plus percentage change in average daily units for the selected
date range shifted back one year versus January 1 of that year through the day
before the shifted period starts. Each total is divided by its inclusive number
of calendar days, including days without sales. A period starting
on January 1 has no preceding accumulation and leaves this indicator empty.
The dates are inclusive; changing dates, company or products refreshes the
indicators from cached sales history. The same positive-base rule applies.

Category and product increase lists show the same three historical growth
indicators, scoped to each line and the calculation product selection. Category
figures include descendant categories and products using the general method or
mixed key-account source. These read-only indicators leave entered increases
unchanged. Save and reload after changing calculation dates or line selections.

Annual trends compare January 1 through yesterday with the same dates last year.
On January 1 they are empty because no complete day is available yet.

General-method increases resolve product overrides first, then category overrides,
then the general increase. When no percentage is specified, use 0% and keep
the historical base quantity unchanged before calendar adjustments.

Export Latest Forecasts to Excel downloads weekly lines from the latest calculated
forecast per method for the current calculation and company, matching Open Latest
Forecasts. Includes product, variant, dates, units, boxes and pallets.
